{
  "dom_id": "step-q325858",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{3!}{{s}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}} & =\\frac{3!}{(s-4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
